    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Deformation bands in porous sandstones are small fault-like structures that accommodate both compaction and shear displacement through localized collapsing of pores and crushing of grains. Their geometry in outcrop is complex but can be resolved by identifying the repetition of simple interaction structures at different scales. The distribution and density of deformation bands in a given locality is controlled by the distance from faults or major structures. The localization and the internal structure of deformation bands depend on the lithologic characteristics of the parent rock units. The gouge material in the deformation bands affects the fluid flow properties of the faulted rock.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
